an is internally inconsistent**: its prose says 9, but its expected test-output strings (e.g., `\"  causes   \"`, `\"  try      X\"`, the spacing in the empty-label continuation test) are arithmetically computed using a width of 7, not 9. With width=9, `\"  causes   \"` would have to be `\"  causes     \"` (13 chars, not 11) and `\"  try      X\"` would have to be `\"  try        X\"` (14 chars, not 12).\n\nThe implementation clearly chose the test-string arithmetic over the prose. That's a defensible interpretation, but it has two consequences:\n\n- **Visual regression vs. existing `formatCheckpointHeader`.** When Task 1.7+ replaces the inline `writeRow` closure in `formatCheckpointHeader` with `metadataRow`/`metadataRows`, every existing `entire explain` user will see one less column of indentation under the labels (`session`, `created`, `author`, `tokens`, `branch`, etc., are all ≤9 chars — `session` and `created` are 7, fitting the new min exactly with no padding; previously they were padded to 9). This is the kind of change reviewers in later tasks may flag as unintended drift unless someone has explicitly decided the new look is preferred.\n- **Test name is misleading downstream.** `TestMetadataRow_PadsLabelToMin7` codifies the new value, which is fine — but anyone reading the plan first and then the code will be confused.\n\n**Recommendation:** Decide explicitly which width is correct, then reconcile. Two paths:\n\n- **Path A (keep min=7):** Accept the visual narrowing, update the plan prose (lines 108, 126, 132 and the code block at 129, 134) to say 7, and add a brief note to the Task 1.1 commit message or PR explaining \"tightening the column from 9 to 7 — `session`/`created`/`author`/`tokens` all fit in 7 and the extra space wasn't carrying its weight.\"\n- **Path B (revert to min=9, matches today):** Change `width := 7` → `width := 9` in both `metadataRow` and `metadataRows`, and update the four test expectations accordingly (test 1: `\"  session    2026-04-30-c4f1\\n\"`; test 4: `\"  causes     \\n    • alpha\\n    • beta\\n  try        X\\n\"`).\n\nI'd lean toward Path A only if the visual change is intentional and previously discussed; otherwise Path B preserves Task 1.1's stated invariant (\"matches existing `formatCheckpointHeader` rendering\") and avoids a surprise diff for users when later tasks land. Please confirm with the planning agent which is intended.\n\n### Important\n\nNone.\n\n### Minor\n\n- **Possible trailing whitespace in continuation rendering.** When a row has a non-empty label but an empty value (e.g., `{Label: \"causes\", Value: \"\"}` in the continuation test), the output is `\"  causes   \\n\"` — i.e., trailing whitespace after the label. This is actually what the plan's expected string requires, and it's necessary for the dim-styled label to render with its full width when followed by content on subsequent lines. But some editors / lint pipelines flag trailing whitespace. Worth being aware of when tests grow; not a blocker.\n- **Doc-comment width number will need updating** if the Critical issue is resolved. The comment at `cmd/entire/cli/status_style.go:126` says \"7-char min-padded label\" and line 136 says \"max(7, widest label)\". If you take Path B above, update these too.\n- **`metadataRowsWithWidth` is unexported but uncommented.** It's a private helper, so it's fine, but a one-line doc-comment explaining the contract (that callers have already computed `width`) would help future maintainers — especially since it's the only place the empty-label branch lives, which is the \"interesting\" behavior.\n- **Test file imports `io` only.** Clean and minimal — good. No assertion library introduced; raw `if got != want` is consistent with the rest of the package's test style.\n\n## Assessment\n\n**Verdict:** Approve with one clarification needed.\n\nThe code is well-structured, lint-clean, and test-covered. The three-method API is exactly the abstraction Task 1.1 promised, and the empty-labe